  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cole shut them down for six scoreless innings. They just couldn't break through until it was gone.",
    "what_this_means": "Gerrit Cole did everything asked of him: six innings, two hits, no runs, the kind of performance that should stack wins. But the Yankees mustered only one run from thirteen runners in scoring position across nine innings, leaving just enough ambiguity in the first six to let Tampa Bay steal the game in the eighth. That's how good pitching dies in baseball. Not by getting shelled, but by arriving at an empty stadium. The bullpen couldn't hold what Cole built. Tim Hill entered in the eighth and gave up three runs in a moment that defined the whole night. Cole's shutdown deserved better. This team had the game and let it slip away in one inning.",
